    "result_text": "Both tests pass and the full suite is green. Here's what I implemented.\n\n## Summary\n\nAdded a structured address form (`addressLine1` / optional `addressLine2`, alongside the existing `city` / `postcode`) while keeping the flat `address` input backward-compatible.\n\n**Contract & persistence**\n- `openapi.yml` \u2014 added `addressLine1` and `addressLine2` to the `OwnerFields` schema (so they appear on both the request DTO and the `Owner` response DTO), and removed `address` from `required` so a structured-only owner is valid. Documented that `address` is returned as the composed, normalized value.\n- `Owner` entity \u2014 added `addressLine1` / `addressLine2` fields, getters/setters, and `toString`.\n- `db/{hsqldb,h2,postgres,mysql}/schema.sql` \u2014 added `address_line1` / `address_line2` columns (tests run on hsqldb).\n\n**Logic (all address composition centralized in one helper \u2014 no duplication)**\n- `Addresses.canonical(line1, line2, flat)` \u2014 the single source of truth: normalized `addressLine1`, plus a space and normalized `addressLine2` when present; falls back to the normalized flat `address` when no structured line1 is given.\n- `ValidateOwnerFields` \u2014 accepts an owner when `canonical(...)` is non-blank, i.e. a non-blank `addressLine1` **or** the flat `address`, plus `city` (earlier minimal owners still accepted).\n- `NormalizeOwnerAddress` \u2014 normalizes whichever fields are supplied (structured lines in place) and sets the flat `address` to the composed canonical form.\n- `ApplyOwner` (PUT) \u2014 updated the same way for consistency.\n\n**Address consumers** (household hash, postcode validation, locality) already key off `lastName`/`postcode`/`city`, which are unchanged shared fields; they read the composed `address` transparently via the entity, so no further changes were needed there.\n\nMapStruct maps the new same-named fields automatically. `Tests run: 2, Failures: 0, Errors: 0 \u2014 BUILD SUCCESS`.",
